---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/use-cases/discount-strategy';
const title = 'Discount strategy for Shopify SMS marketing';
const desc = 'Define eligible offers, protect contribution margin, coordinate channels, and test whether discounts change buying behavior.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/use-cases" class="text-sm font-bold text-orange-700">← All use cases</a>
      <p class="badge mt-6">Discount strategy</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">Make the offer earn its margin</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">A redeemed code shows that someone used an incentive. It does not show that the incentive created an order. Plan discounts around eligibility, checkout behavior, and contribution from everyone assigned to the offer, including people who never buy.</p>
      <div class="mt-9 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/apps">Explore apps</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/guides/offer-discipline">Offer discipline</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12 space-y-8">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Write an offer contract before writing the text</h2>
      <p>Give each offer an identifier and immutable version. Record its purpose, qualifying customers, products and variants, markets, currency, minimum spend, exclusions, usage limits, start and expiry instants with timezone, and allowed combinations. State whether eligibility is checked when the message is queued, when it is sent, or at checkout; these can produce different audiences. A send-time audience match must never be presented as a promise that any later cart will qualify.</p>
      <p>Separate public codes from customer-bound eligibility. A unique-looking code is not proof that only the intended shopper can redeem it. Check the actual checkout restrictions and decide what happens when a code is shared, a customer changes email, or a cart moves between devices. Keep the version used for each exposure so a later rule change does not rewrite the history of what was promised.</p>
      <p>Shopify documents that combination settings on each discount must allow the combination. Its supported combinations have eligibility conditions. Review the <a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/discounts/discount-combinations">official discount-combination documentation</a> and test the store's actual checkout. Do not infer stacking behavior from two valid codes considered separately.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Display material exclusions, the qualifying threshold, and an unambiguous expiry alongside the offer.</li>
        <li>Check sale items, subscriptions, gift cards, bundles, shipping offers, and loyalty rewards where relevant to your catalog.</li>
        <li>Choose a rule for expired links and withdrawn offers. Do not silently extend an expired promise or switch its terms.</li>
        <li>Authorize who can change or disable the offer, and retain the prior version and reason.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Compare contribution, not just revenue</h2>
      <p>Start with order receipts after discounts and refunds, excluding taxes collected for remittance. Subtract product costs net of recoverable returned inventory, fulfillment, payment costs, shipping subsidy, variable support and return handling, and message costs. If receipts already reflect the discount and refund, do not subtract those amounts again. If your ledger starts with gross sales instead, reconcile those adjustments once before calculating contribution.</p>
      <p>Hypothetical worksheet: an order has $100 in merchandise before a $10 discount and a later $20 refund. Net receipts are $70. If the remaining variable costs total $45, contribution is $25. Subtracting the $10 and $20 a second time would incorrectly produce a $5 loss. This illustration excludes tax and assumes the $45 already reflects the return's inventory treatment.</p>
      <p>Revenue can rise while contribution falls if shoppers who would pay full price take the code. Include assigned non-buyers and failed sends in the experiment denominator. Keep recurring platform commitments, implementation work, and other fixed costs in a separate rollout budget; compare incremental contribution with the additional fixed cost before scaling. Use <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">pricing economics</a> to build a volume-sensitive cost model from current quotes.</p>
      <p>Follow the cohort beyond the first order. Track later full-price purchases, repeat contribution, returns, opt-outs, and time until the next purchase over a window chosen before the test. A short promotion can pull an order forward without adding demand. Avoid labeling people as permanent bargain hunters from one redemption.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Keep identity, permission, and exits separate from eligibility</h2>
      <p>A shopper may qualify for a discount without having permission for a promotional text. Require the appropriate channel permission, matching capture purpose, and applicable sending restrictions independently of offer eligibility. Email permission does not establish SMS permission. Use the <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">consent guide</a> as an operational starting point and get qualified advice for the markets you serve.</p>
      <p>Use a stable customer identity where available, while accounting for shared numbers and profile merges. Do not expose a private customer offer merely because an anonymous browser resembles a known buyer. Carry redemption history, experiment assignment, withdrawal records, and suppression scope through merges; unresolved identity conflicts should pause targeted sends rather than grant new entitlement.</p>
      <p>Recheck permission, purchase state, support pauses, expiry, and eligibility immediately before dispatch. A purchase should close the relevant cart or offer reminder; define separately whether unrelated promotions remain eligible. Honor withdrawal in its applicable scope and apply a temporary broader suppression when the scope is uncertain pending resolution. Replaying an event must not resurrect an exited journey.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Reserve inventory and the customer's attention</h2>
      <p>Decide whether a discount is meant to clear surplus or stimulate demand for replenishable stock. Check sellable variant inventory and fulfillment constraints before exposure. A message does not reserve stock unless your checkout actually does so. State genuine limits without manufactured scarcity, and stop queued promotion when the qualifying inventory is unavailable.</p>
      <p>Coordinate welcome codes, cart recovery, loyalty rewards, winback, subscription renewals, and broadcasts through a shared offer ledger. At dispatch, reserve the permitted exposure atomically across journeys and channels so simultaneous workers cannot both claim the slot. Define which offer wins and what happens to the loser; delaying a message must preserve its original expiry rather than restart a countdown.</p>
      <p>A unified email and SMS setup can fit when one team can enforce common eligibility and exits. Separate senders can fit when the team can synchronize suppression and offer ownership reliably. An email-led program can fit when terms need explanation and the audience permits that channel. Compare <a class="link" href="/compare">app options</a> against these requirements using a live demonstration; a supplier name alone does not establish that the coordination works.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Test the incentive separately from the reminder</h2>
      <p>Where sample size permits, randomly assign eligible customers to no promotional message, a comparable reminder without an incentive, and a reminder with the offer. Keep assignment stable across channels and profile merges. Specify outcomes, observation window, stopping rules, and meaningful effect size before starting. Retain non-buyers and delivery failures in their assigned groups; redemption-only analysis selects people after treatment.</p>
      <p>Report contribution per assigned eligible customer and the difference between groups with uncertainty intervals. Hypothetically, $1,200 contribution across 1,000 assigned customers is $1.20 each; $1,000 across 1,000 controls is $1.00 each, a $0.20 observed difference before additional fixed costs. This arithmetic alone cannot establish a reliable effect. Sparse orders, highly variable baskets, and delayed refunds may leave the result inconclusive.</p>
      <p>Record public-code leakage and competing offers. Shared scarce inventory can make treatment purchases prevent control purchases, distorting a customer-level test. Consider a design with suitable inventory separation or specialist statistical advice; report this limitation rather than attributing every difference to the discount. Keep results for full-price buyers visible so aggregate revenue cannot hide displacement.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Recover checkout failures without escalating the discount</h2>
      <p>Exercise links on mobile and across the checkout paths you support. Check logged-in and guest baskets, currency, variant exclusions, thresholds after cart changes, competing automatic discounts, and expiry boundaries. Capture the offer version, cart conditions, and rejection reason without storing unnecessary personal data.</p>
      <p>If an advertised offer fails for an eligible customer, pause affected sends, reproduce the error, and provide an accurate support path. If the cart is ineligible, explain the failed condition; do not issue a larger incentive automatically. Corrective outreach still needs its own permission and classification review. Existing purchases and withdrawals must remain exits during recovery.</p>
      <p>Use an idempotency key for each intended exposure and retain provider acceptance state. A timeout may mean the provider accepted a send; reconcile before retrying rather than sending again on uncertainty. After an outage, re-evaluate the original offer version, expiry, stock, permission, and purchase state. Discard stale queued messages instead of dumping the backlog into a new campaign.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Proposed acceptance fixtures</h2>
      <p>These are hypothetical scenarios for a store's implementation review, not merchant results or promises about any app.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Offer version changes:</strong> a queued message references version A; an operator publishes B with a higher threshold. Proposed pass: A is either honored under its documented rules or its send is cancelled, with a recorded decision and no silent substitution.</li>
        <li><strong>Stacking collision:</strong> a qualifying basket already has a loyalty benefit. Proposed pass: checkout applies the configured allowed combination or explains the conflict; the SMS does not promise savings the tested basket cannot receive.</li>
        <li><strong>Identity and withdrawal:</strong> two profiles merge after an SMS opt-out. Proposed pass: the merged profile retains the withdrawal and original experiment assignment; the merge does not trigger another offer.</li>
        <li><strong>Purchase race:</strong> checkout completion arrives while a cart-discount reminder is queued. Proposed pass: the dispatch eligibility check closes the relevant reminder and a duplicate purchase event cannot reopen it.</li>
        <li><strong>Inventory and journey race:</strong> welcome and winback workers target the same customer as the qualifying variant sells out. Proposed pass: shared exposure reservation prevents duplicate promotions, and the stock check cancels the unavailable offer.</li>
        <li><strong>Checkout rejection:</strong> a cart falls below the minimum after an item is removed. Proposed pass: the threshold failure is explained, no replacement discount is generated automatically, and support can identify the offer version.</li>
        <li><strong>Uncertain delivery:</strong> the provider times out after accepting a message. Proposed pass: reconciliation prevents a duplicate send and an expired offer is not replayed after recovery.</li>
        <li><strong>Refund accounting:</strong> the illustrative $100 order receives the $10 discount and $20 refund with $45 variable costs. Proposed pass: contribution is $25, and the assigned customer remains in the original experiment denominator.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Continue with <a class="link" href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">email and SMS coordination</a>, <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">cart recovery</a>, and <a class="link" href="/guides/campaign-restraint">campaign restraint</a> when setting shared dispatch rules.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
